   "text": {
    "L1": "After the Harappan cities declined, people lived mainly in villages for many centuries. Around 600 BCE, cities grew again, especially in the Ganga plains.",
    "L2": "A janapada was the land where a jana, or people, settled. Some janapadas became powerful mahajanapadas.",
    "L3": "Iron tools helped clear land and grow extra food, called a surplus. The surplus supported crafts, trade, taxes, armies and fortified capitals.",
    "L4": "Some mahajanapadas were kingdoms ruled by a raja. Others were gana-sanghas, where many leaders made decisions together in an assembly.",
    "L5": "Magadha grew strong and had the fortified capital Rajagriha. We learn about this period from texts, archaeology and punch-marked coins.",
    "L6": "Remember the BCE order: a bigger number means earlier. So 600 BCE is earlier than 500 BCE.",
    "L7": "Put the key dates in order from earliest to latest. Then check your understanding."
   },
